[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-20", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 20 — the plan was the query; whole-relation estimate cached per index version, 64-slot row 1533 → 2027 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-20.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 20 - the plan was the query: the whole-relation estimate cached per index version\n\n> With every read at the wire at 8 slots (iteration 19), the wider slot\n> columns measure the SUT's own capacity: 1533 ops/s at 64 slots with\n> 2048 failing. Question: what does the SUT spend per operation? Judge:\n> bench/benchmark.edn v2, rung SF1 subset, origin :cross-host (simeon),\n> mix IC02/07/08/09, impl :datalog.\n\n## Generate\n\n  Measured in-process on simeon before touching anything: IC08 total\n  8.98 ms, of which the PLAN was 9.19 ms (the estimates alone: replyOf\n  2.9 ms, hasCreator 4.6, creationDate 0.7). datalog-query-plan estimates\n  every clause of every query, and a clause with only its attribute bound\n  costs a reduce over every subject bucket of that attribute's :aevt map\n  -- 51,932 and 86,932 buckets here. Iteration 17 had put this at 2 ms on\n  a 2-pass warm-up; measured properly it is the whole operation.\n\n  Prediction: cache the whole-relation count per attribute, keyed by\n  identity of the :aevt inner map (a value; a transaction touching the\n  attribute replaces it, one not touching it keeps it) -> IC08 in-process\n  under 0.5 ms; 8 slots unchanged (the wire); 64 slots 1533 -> >= 2500\n  with 2048 passing.\n\n## Rank\n\n    in-process                         before      after\n    plan (IC08 shape)                   9.193      0.019 ms\n    ic08 total                          8.982      0.167\n    ic07                                1.5        0.4\n    ic02 empty / nonempty               0.1 / 2.4  0.1 / 2.5   (the drive's walk, not the plan)\n    ic09 empty                          2.8        2.8         (the drive's independent half)\n\n    served, cross-host, impl datalog, 392/392 agreement:\n    slots  rate  on-time%  thru/s   ic02  ic07  ic08  ic09\n      8     256  100.00    255.9   12.2  10.4   9.9  15.4   (unchanged: the wire)\n     64    1536  100.00   1531.9\n     64    2048   98.65   2027.0   16.4  15.6  15.2  19.9   <- 1533 -> 2027\n     64    3072   89.40   2903.8\n    128    2048   refused: 80 of 40,960 operations failed (fetch failed) -- exit 2\n\n  Test: same db hits the same entry; a transaction on another attribute\n  keeps the hit; one on the attribute moves the count and replaces the\n  entry; a cache that never invalidates fails it. Suite 262/804/0.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Prediction held on direction and on 2048 passing, short on size:\n    1.32x, not 1.6x. At 3072 the SUT delivered 2,904 ops/s but 11% of\n    operations started late -- start-delay p95 of a second with per-op\n    latency at 17 ms means the DRIVER's 64 slots were the queue\n    (64 / 0.017 s is ~3,800 in flight; the schedule outran the slots).\n    128 slots hit connection failures under the burst and the driver\n    refused to measure them. The next ceiling is the driver and the wire.\n  - The finding is the same shape as iterations 17 and 18: a correct,\n    necessary piece of the engine whose cost was invisible from the query\n    it served, found by measuring the layer below the symptom. The plan\n    was 100% of a 9 ms operation and 0% of the answer.\n  - The SUT now spends under a millisecond in the engine on IC07/IC08 and\n    ~3 ms on IC02/IC09; per operation the HTTP hop, EDN parsing and the\n    drive's independent half are what is left.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH on the 64-slot Datalog\ncolumn (1533 -> 2027); 8-slot column unchanged at the wire (:no-growth,\nby construction). Ratio to the audited No.1: 69 at 64 slots, raw.\n\nSeeds for iteration 21, in order:\n\n  - S10 (wire and driver): the driver is a single nbb event loop on a\n    host at load 20-45 over a 10-14 ms rtt. A driver on the fleet LAN\n    (rtt ~1 ms, an idle host) is a different origin column and would show\n    the SUT's own ceiling; the 128-slot connection failures need a\n    backlog on the JDK HttpServer (currently the system default) and a\n    keep-alive pool sized to the slots.\n  - S9 (independent half): the dense IC09's ~3 ms is the two-hop\n    friend-of-friend join; as sets it is ~1 ms.\n  - S3 (mix): short reads IS1-IS7 -- point lookups the datom plane\n    answers in microseconds; the mix would move toward LDBC's.\n  - S2 (rung): full SF1 needs the per-quad footprint measured first\n    (1.3 GB heap for 810k quads does not fit 16 GiB at 16.5M).\n"}]
